I've written the critic review for the Olivia Dean draft to `docs/content-analysis/pipeline-logs/2026-10-23_06-00-05-811_olivia-dean/review-critic.json`. It passes the pipeline's review schema, its hashes match the request, and every passage it cites matches the draft exactly. I changed no other files, didn't read the other reviewers' files, and published nothing.

**Verdict:** the draft is honest about its limits, but its main idea ("she keeps handing the moment back") only holds up because the article counts evidence it has already given away and trims sources that cut against it. The review has 6 major and 2 minor findings:

1. **The Type 3 test doesn't test what it says.** The article says a Three protects image while a Two protects relationships. But every example it uses (the *Messy* title, "wangled", the failed LA sessions) is about being candid rather than polished. None shows her choosing a relationship over her image.
2. **Deleting her social apps is used against Type 3 only.** The full ELLE quote ends "I don't think you're supposed to know everyone's opinion about you." That cuts against a Type 2 reading just as much.
3. **The Ticketmaster "fight" carried little risk.** Ticketmaster reposted her message the same day and said it supports "artists' ability to set the terms" of resale. Live Nation's CEO publicly backed her within the week. So the episode doesn't show costly conflict, and it doesn't count against Type 9.
4. **The hook and title rest on one anecdote.** The article itself concedes that a Three could have made the Ticketmaster move and that any headliner would apologize in Auckland, then counts both for Type 2 anyway. That leaves the champagne, given to the ELLE writer covering her. The article doesn't use that writer's own reading of the moment, that Dean "romanticizes life", and gives no baseline for Grammy speeches.
5. **The closing question has already been answered.** The article ends by saying her next goals would settle the read. In the same Apple Music interview it quotes, she named them right away: headlining Glastonbury and touring South America and India. Those point to Types 3 and 7, not Type 2.
6. **The strongest Type 7 evidence is missing.** Numéro has her saying "what I really fear is the day I will stop having fun." The same interview has "I cry in private, rarely in front of people," which complicates the claim that she moves toward pain.
7. *(minor)* **The "selfish" pattern rests on two uses with opposite tone.** The full Time Out quote ends "Who gives a fuck what other people think about it?", which is defiant, not guilty.
8. *(minor)* **One section heading is her own lyric.** "I'm too scared to belong to anyone" echoes a line from her song "Something Inbetween", per Genius. Nobody has yet listened to the audio to confirm that Dean, not the interviewer, says it.

The review also protects five passages worth keeping, including the line that calling her boundary talk "Two growth" would make the theory impossible to lose. It keeps both risk flags on: contested typing and sensitive material. I found no misquotes in any source I checked. Those checks used the page texts and transcripts saved during the research run, plus one live lyrics lookup, not fresh visits or audio. Both reasons the full six-reviewer panel was triggered (contested typing, sensitive material) still hold.
